/* First Light: the teachings of tao. Generated by .scripts/plans/build-plans.js from
   .scripts/plans/teachings/tao.json; do not edit by hand. */
FLTextPut("teachings", "tao", {"plan":"tao","div":"bb6ff32525","days":[{"d":1,"at":[0],"ref":"Tao Te Ching 1","key":"(Conceived of as) having no name, it is the Originator of heaven and earth; (conceived of as) having a name, it is the Mother of all things.","s":"Following the chapter’s opening on the enduring Tao and name, the verse sets out two aspects; Wang Bi, on chapter 1, reads both as the Tao, which begins things while they lack form and name and rears them as their mother once formed and named.","by":"Wang Bi"},{"d":2,"at":[1],"ref":"Tao Te Ching 2","key":"All in the world know the beauty of the beautiful, and in doing this they have (the idea of) what ugliness is; they all know the skill of the skilful, and in doing this they have (the idea of) what the want of skill is.","s":"Opening a chapter that sets opposites side by side, the verse gives the first two pairs; Wang Bi, on chapter 2, reads beauty and ugliness as the heart’s delight and loathing, like joy and anger, which share one root, so neither can be singled out.","by":"Wang Bi"},{"d":3,"at":[2],"ref":"Tao Te Ching 3","key":"Therefore the sage, in the exercise of his government, empties their minds, fills their bellies, weakens their wills, and strengthens their bones.","s":"After counsel to keep the people from rivalry and theft, the verse turns to the sage’s rule; the Heshang Gong commentary, on chapter 3, reads it as governing state and self alike, the mind cleared of cravings, the bones firmed by husbanding the vital essence.","by":"the Heshang Gong commentary"},{"d":4,"at":[3],"ref":"Tao Te Ching 4","key":"We should blunt our sharp points, and unravel the complications of things; we should attemper our brightness, and bring ourselves into agreement with the obscurity of others.","s":"Between images of the Tao’s emptiness and stillness, the chapter turns to conduct; the Heshang Gong commentary, on chapter 4, takes it as checking the keen pursuit of merit and fame, untying knotted resentment, and sharing the common dust rather than standing apart.","by":"the Heshang Gong commentary"},{"d":5,"at":[4],"ref":"Tao Te Ching 5","key":"Heaven and earth do not act from (the impulse of) any wish to be benevolent; they deal with all things as the dogs of grass are dealt with.","s":"The chapter opens here, then says the same of the sages; Wang Bi, on chapter 5, reads it as heaven and earth making nothing and leaving all things to order themselves: earth grows no grass for beasts, yet beasts eat grass.","by":"Wang Bi"},{"d":6,"at":[5],"ref":"Tao Te Ching 6","key":"The valley spirit dies not, aye the same; The female mystery thus do we name.","s":"The chapter opens here, then turns to a gate and root; the Heshang Gong commentary, on chapter 6, reads the valley as nourishing the spirits of the five viscera, and the female mystery as heaven and earth, in a person the nose and mouth.","by":"the Heshang Gong commentary"},{"d":7,"at":[6],"ref":"Tao Te Ching 7","key":"Therefore the sage puts his own person last, and yet it is found in the foremost place; he treats his person as if it were foreign to him, and yet that person is preserved.","s":"After heaven and earth, enduring by not living for themselves, the chapter turns to the sage; the Heshang Gong commentary, on chapter 7, reads this as putting others first, taking little for himself and giving much, so the people love him as father and mother.","by":"the Heshang Gong commentary"},{"d":8,"at":[7],"ref":"Tao Te Ching 8","key":"And when (one with the highest excellence) does not wrangle (about his low position), no one finds fault with him.","s":"The verse closes a chapter that likens the highest excellence to water; the Heshang Gong commentary, on chapter 8, takes the not wrangling as water’s way of going along with people, stopping when dammed and flowing when released, so no one under heaven blames it.","by":"the Heshang Gong commentary"},{"d":9,"at":[8],"ref":"Tao Te Ching 9","key":"When the work is done, and one’s name is becoming distinguished, to withdraw into obscurity is the way of Heaven.","s":"After warnings against fullness, sharpness, treasure and arrogance, the chapter ends on success; the Heshang Gong commentary, on chapter 9, reads it as warning that whoever achieves and does not step down meets harm, as the noon sun moves on and the full moon wanes.","by":"the Heshang Gong commentary"},{"d":10,"at":[9],"ref":"Tao Te Ching 10","key":"(The Tao) produces (all things) and nourishes them; it produces them and does not claim them as its own; it does all, and yet does not boast of it; it presides over all, and yet does not control them. This is what is called ‘The mysterious Quality’ (of the Tao).","s":"Ending a chapter on the souls, the breath and ruling the state, the verse sums up; Wang Bi, on chapter 10, reads producing as not blocking the source of things, nourishing as not restraining their nature, and the mystery as virtue with no master.","by":"Wang Bi"},{"d":11,"at":[10],"ref":"Tao Te Ching 11","key":"The thirty spokes unite in the one nave; but it is on the empty space (for the axle), that the use of the wheel depends.","s":"Opening a chapter of three images, with vessel and room to follow, the verse gives the first; Wang Bi, on chapter 11, reads the nave as gathering its spokes through its non-being, which can receive things, and so holding the many together.","by":"Wang Bi"},{"d":12,"at":[11],"ref":"Tao Te Ching 12","key":"Therefore the sage seeks to satisfy (the craving of) the belly, and not the (insatiable longing of the) eyes. He puts from him the latter, and prefers to seek the former.","s":"After the chapter warns how colours, notes, flavours, the hunt and rare objects harm people, the verse draws its lesson; Wang Bi, on chapter 12, reads serving the belly as using things to nourish oneself, and serving the eyes as letting things enslave one.","by":"Wang Bi"},{"d":13,"at":[12],"ref":"Tao Te Ching 13","key":"Therefore he who would administer the kingdom, honouring it as he honours his own person, may be employed to govern it, and he who would administer it with the love which he bears to his own person may be entrusted with it.","s":"Closing a chapter on favour and disgrace, the verse turns to government; the Heshang Gong commentary, on chapter 13, takes the first ruler as prizing himself and scorning others, ruling only briefly, and the second as loving himself for the people’s sake, long without blame.","by":"the Heshang Gong commentary"},{"d":14,"at":[13],"ref":"Tao Te Ching 14","key":"When we can lay hold of the Tao of old to direct the things of the present day, and are able to know it as it was of old in the beginning, this is called (unwinding) the clue of Tao.","s":"Closing a chapter on the invisible, the verse joins past and present; Wang Bi, on chapter 14, takes that Tao as the formless, nameless ancestor of all things, through which every age achieves order however customs change, so the ancient beginning is knowable now.","by":"Wang Bi"},{"d":15,"at":[14],"ref":"Tao Te Ching 15","key":"Shrinking looked they like those who wade through a stream in winter; irresolute like those who are afraid of all around them; grave like a guest (in awe of his host); evanescent like ice that is melting away; unpretentious like wood that has not been fashioned into anything; vacant like a valley, and dull like muddy water.","s":"Describing the skilful masters of old, the verse strings seven likenesses; the Heshang Gong commentary, on chapter 15, takes the ice as shedding feeling and desire, the wood as spirit kept within without ornament, and the muddy water as merging with the crowd.","by":"the Heshang Gong commentary"},{"d":16,"at":[15],"ref":"Tao Te Ching 16","key":"All things alike go through their processes of activity, and (then) we see them return (to their original state).","s":"After a call for vacancy and stillness, the chapter turns to things in motion; Wang Bi, on chapter 16, takes the return as seen through emptiness and stillness, to which all things go back at last, since being arises from emptiness and movement from stillness.","by":"Wang Bi"},{"d":17,"at":[16],"ref":"Tao Te Ching 17","key":"In the next age they loved them and praised them. In the next they feared them; in the next they despised them.","s":"Tracing rule from the highest antiquity, the chapter names three later ages; Wang Bi, on chapter 17, takes them as lesser rulers: one who, unable to govern by non-action, bestows favours, one who relies on power, and one who rules by cleverness and is evaded.","by":"Wang Bi"},{"d":18,"at":[17],"ref":"Tao Te Ching 18","key":"When the Great Tao (Way or Method) ceased to be observed, benevolence and righteousness came into vogue.","s":"Opening a chapter where virtues appear as order fails, the verse gives the first; the Heshang Gong commentary, on chapter 18, reads the Great Tao’s age as one of filial sons and good faith, when benevolence and righteousness went unseen until evil and rebellion arose.","by":"the Heshang Gong commentary"},{"d":19,"at":[18],"ref":"Tao Te Ching 19","key":"If we could renounce our sageness and discard our wisdom, it would be better for the people a hundredfold.","s":"This opens the chapter’s three renunciations, which close on simple views and plain courses; the Heshang Gong commentary, on chapter 19, reads renouncing sageness as giving up the sages’ making to return to the beginning, and discarding wisdom as return to non-action.","by":"the Heshang Gong commentary"},{"d":20,"at":[19],"ref":"Tao Te Ching 20","key":"The multitude of men look satisfied and pleased; as if enjoying a full banquet, as if mounted on a tower in spring. I alone seem listless and still, my desires having as yet given no indication of their presence.","s":"After renouncing learning, the chapter sets the crowd against its speaker; the Heshang Gong commentary, on chapter 20, reads the crowd’s pleasure as dissolute desire, a hungry man longing for a feast, never satisfied, and the stillness as calm, with no sign of passion yet.","by":"the Heshang Gong commentary"},{"d":21,"at":[20],"ref":"Tao Te Ching 21","key":"How know I that it is so with all the beauties of existing things? By this (nature of the Tao).","s":"Closing a chapter on the elusive Tao, the text asks and answers; Wang Bi, on chapter 21, reads the question as how all things are known to begin in non-being, and ‘this’ as the account above of the nameless, through which all is completed.","by":"Wang Bi"},{"d":22,"at":[21],"ref":"Tao Te Ching 22","key":"The partial becomes complete; the crooked, straight; the empty, full; the worn out, new.","s":"Reversals open a chapter on the sage free from self-display; Wang Bi, on chapter 22, reads them as not displaying oneself, not holding oneself right, not boasting and not vaunting oneself, which leave one whole, one’s rightness plain, one’s merit kept and one’s virtue lasting.","by":"Wang Bi"},{"d":23,"at":[22],"ref":"Tao Te Ching 23","key":"Abstaining from speech marks him who is obeying the spontaneity of his nature.","s":"A chapter on passing storms and on following the Tao opens with the verse; Wang Bi, on chapter 23, takes the speech as faint, unheard like the Tao’s flavourless words, and reads it as the utmost speech of what is so of itself.","by":"Wang Bi"},{"d":24,"at":[23],"ref":"Tao Te Ching 24","key":"He who stands on his tiptoes does not stand firm; he who stretches his legs does not walk (easily).","s":"Two images open a chapter on self-display and self-conceit; the Heshang Gong commentary, on chapter 24, takes the first as one greedy for power and fame, who cannot stand long, and the second as striding over others in self-importance till the many block the way.","by":"the Heshang Gong commentary"},{"d":25,"at":[24],"ref":"Tao Te Ching 25","key":"Man takes his law from the Earth; the Earth takes its law from Heaven; Heaven takes its law from the Tao. The law of the Tao is its being what it is.","s":"Closing a chapter on the formless thing before Heaven and Earth, the verse links four in a chain; Wang Bi, on chapter 25, reads each as not going against the next, and the Tao as going against nothing that is so of itself.","by":"Wang Bi"},{"d":26,"at":[25],"ref":"Tao Te Ching 26","key":"Gravity is the root of lightness; stillness, the ruler of movement.","s":"The chapter opens here, then turns to a prince and his baggage waggons; the Heshang Gong commentary, on chapter 26, reads it for the ruler, not honoured without gravity and losing authority without stillness, and for the self, the spirit lost and the body endangered.","by":"the Heshang Gong commentary"},{"d":27,"at":[26],"ref":"Tao Te Ching 27","key":"In the same way the sage is always skilful at saving men, and so he does not cast away any man; he is always skilful at saving things, and so he does not cast away anything.","s":"After five skilful figures, the chapter turns to the sage; Wang Bi, on chapter 27, reads it as setting no forms and names to check things and no path of advancement to cast off the unworthy, but aiding all things to be so of themselves.","by":"Wang Bi"},{"d":28,"at":[27],"ref":"Tao Te Ching 28","key":"Who knows his manhood’s strength, Yet still his female feebleness maintains; As to one channel flow the many drains, All come to him, yea, all beneath the sky.","s":"The verse opens three stanzas that each end in a return to simplicity; the Heshang Gong commentary, on chapter 28, reads it as one who, knowing his eminence, keeps lowly and gentle, so the world comes to him like water into a deep ravine.","by":"the Heshang Gong commentary"},{"d":29,"at":[28],"ref":"Tao Te Ching 29","key":"Hence the sage puts away excessive effort, extravagance, and easy indulgence.","s":"The chapter draws its lesson from how things turn about; the Heshang Gong commentary, on chapter 29, reads the three as craving for music and beauty, fine dress and food, and palaces and terraces, whose putting away, with non-action, lets the world transform itself.","by":"the Heshang Gong commentary"},{"d":30,"at":[29],"ref":"Tao Te Ching 30","key":"He strikes it as a matter of necessity; he strikes it, but not from a wish for mastery.","s":"The chapter warns against mastery by arms and speaks of one decisive blow; Wang Bi, on chapter 30, reads it as troops used only to relieve distress when the times allow no choice, and only to put down violence and disorder, never for strength.","by":"Wang Bi"},{"d":31,"at":[30],"ref":"Tao Te Ching 31","key":"He who has killed multitudes of men should weep for them with the bitterest grief; and the victor in battle has his place (rightly) according to those rites.","s":"The chapter ends on the dead of war; the Heshang Gong commentary, on chapter 31, reads the weeping as grief that thin virtue, unable to transform by the Tao, harmed the innocent, and the victor’s white mourning as fitting one who strikes only of necessity.","by":"the Heshang Gong commentary"},{"d":32,"at":[31],"ref":"Tao Te Ching 32","key":"Though in its primordial simplicity it may be small, the whole world dares not deal with (one embodying) it as a minister.","s":"Having called the Tao nameless, the chapter turns to its simplicity; Wang Bi, on chapter 32, reads simplicity as leaning to no side and close to nothing, so that none can employ it as the clever, the brave and the strong are employed.","by":"Wang Bi"},{"d":33,"at":[32],"ref":"Tao Te Ching 33","key":"He who knows other men is discerning; he who knows himself is intelligent. He who overcomes others is strong; he who overcomes himself is mighty.","s":"The verse opens a chapter of paired sayings that ends on longevity; Wang Bi, on chapter 33, reads knowing and overcoming others as mere cleverness and force, falling short of turning both upon oneself, whose strength nothing can diminish.","by":"Wang Bi"},{"d":34,"at":[33],"ref":"Tao Te Ching 34","key":"Hence the sage is able (in the same way) to accomplish his great achievements. It is through his not making himself great that he can accomplish them.","s":"Ending a chapter on the Tao, which claims no credit or lordship, the text turns to the sage; the Heshang Gong commentary, on chapter 34, reads him as hiding virtue and name, as the Tao does, and teaching by his own example, without words.","by":"the Heshang Gong commentary"},{"d":35,"at":[34],"ref":"Tao Te Ching 35","key":"To him who holds in his hands the Great Image (of the invisible Tao), the whole world repairs. Men resort to him, and receive no hurt, but (find) rest, peace, and the feeling of ease.","s":"The chapter, on the Tao’s unending use, opens with the Great Image; Wang Bi, on chapter 35, reads it as the mother of heaven’s images, neither cold nor warm, embracing all things and harming none, so that the world goes to a ruler holding it.","by":"Wang Bi"},{"d":36,"at":[35],"ref":"Tao Te Ching 36","key":"Fishes should not be taken from the deep; instruments for the profit of a state should not be shown to the people.","s":"After the soft overcomes the hard, the chapter ends with two warnings; Wang Bi, on chapter 36, reads the instruments as ordering things by their nature, not by punishment, and showing them as ruling by punishment, which fails like a fish out of the deep.","by":"Wang Bi"},{"d":37,"at":[36],"ref":"Tao Te Ching 37","key":"Simplicity without a name Is free from all external aim. With no desire, at rest and still, All things go right as of their will.","s":"Closing a chapter on kings keeping the Tao, the verse meets desire with simplicity; the Heshang Gong commentary, on chapter 37, reads it as rulers steadying the people by the Tao and its virtue till they too stop desiring and the world settles itself.","by":"the Heshang Gong commentary"},{"d":38,"at":[37],"ref":"Tao Te Ching 38","key":"(Those who) possessed in highest degree the attributes (of the Tao) did not (seek) to show them, and therefore they possessed them (in fullest measure). (Those who) possessed in a lower degree those attributes (sought how) not to lose them, and therefore they did not possess them (in fullest measure).","s":"The chapter begins its account of decline from the Tao; Wang Bi, on chapter 38, reads the highest as using only the Tao, gaining without seeking and having virtue without its name, and the lower as seeking, its gains sure to be lost.","by":"Wang Bi"},{"d":39,"at":[38],"ref":"Tao Te Ching 39","key":"Thus it is that dignity finds its (firm) root in its (previous) meanness, and what is lofty finds its stability in the lowness (from which it rises).","s":"After its warnings of ruin, the chapter states a rule; the Heshang Gong commentary, on chapter 39, reads it as telling whoever seeks honour to take the lowly as root, as a wall rises from a low base and topples if that base is weak.","by":"the Heshang Gong commentary"},{"d":40,"at":[39],"ref":"Tao Te Ching 40","key":"The movement of the Tao By contraries proceeds; And weakness marks the course Of Tao’s mighty deeds.","s":"Opening a chapter on where all things come from, the verse tells how the Tao moves and works; Wang Bi, on chapter 40, reads the movement as reversal, the high founded on the low, the noble on the humble, and non-being the use of being.","by":"Wang Bi"},{"d":41,"at":[40],"ref":"Tao Te Ching 41","key":"The Tao is hidden, and has no name; but it is the Tao which is skilful at imparting (to all things what they need) and making them complete.","s":"Closing a chapter of sayings on the Tao’s seeming opposites, the verse turns to the Tao itself; Wang Bi, on chapter 41, reads each excellence in the sayings as its work, hidden and nameless because things are completed by it yet its form goes unseen.","by":"Wang Bi"},{"d":42,"at":[41],"ref":"Tao Te Ching 42","key":"The Tao produced One; One produced Two; Two produced Three; Three produced All things.","s":"Before turning to kings and their humble titles, the chapter opens on a sequence of births; the Heshang Gong commentary, on chapter 42, takes the Two as yin and yang, and the Three as harmonious, clear and turbid breaths that become heaven, earth and man.","by":"the Heshang Gong commentary"},{"d":43,"at":[42],"ref":"Tao Te Ching 43","key":"The softest thing in the world dashes against and overcomes the hardest; that which has no (substantial) existence enters where there is no crevice.","s":"Opening a chapter on the advantage of non-action, the verse sets soft against hard; the Heshang Gong commentary, on chapter 43, takes the softest as water and the hardest as metal and stone, and what has no existence as the Tao, without form or substance.","by":"the Heshang Gong commentary"},{"d":44,"at":[43],"ref":"Tao Te Ching 44","key":"Who is content Needs fear no shame. Who knows to stop Incurs no blame. From danger free Long live shall he.","s":"A chapter weighing fame and wealth against life closes with counsel; the Heshang Gong commentary, on chapter 44, takes contentment as renouncing profit and desire, and lasting long as won by governing the self without wearying the spirit and the state without disturbing the people.","by":"the Heshang Gong commentary"},{"d":45,"at":[44],"ref":"Tao Te Ching 45","key":"Purity and stillness give the correct law to all under heaven.","s":"Ending a chapter where great things seem their opposites, the verse makes stillness the rule; Wang Bi, on chapter 45, reads stillness as keeping whole the truth of things and agitation as violating their nature, so only purity and stillness reach the chapter’s great things.","by":"Wang Bi"},{"d":46,"at":[45],"ref":"Tao Te Ching 46","key":"When the Tao prevails in the world, they send back their swift horses to (draw) the dung-carts. When the Tao is disregarded in the world, the war-horses breed in the border lands.","s":"Opening a chapter in praise of contentment, the verse sets peace beside war; Wang Bi, on chapter 46, reads the peaceful world as content and knowing when to stop, each tending what is within, and the warring one as insatiable greed seeking outside.","by":"Wang Bi"},{"d":47,"at":[46],"ref":"Tao Te Ching 47","key":"Without going outside his door, one understands (all that takes place) under the sky; without looking out from his window, one sees the Tao of Heaven.","s":"In a chapter on knowing without travelling, the verse keeps the knower indoors; the Heshang Gong commentary, on chapter 47, reads it as the sage knowing others’ persons and homes through his own, and Heaven’s way through man’s, the two ways being the same.","by":"the Heshang Gong commentary"},{"d":48,"at":[47],"ref":"Tao Te Ching 48","key":"He who devotes himself to learning (seeks) from day to day to increase (his knowledge); he who devotes himself to the Tao (seeks) from day to day to diminish (his doing).","s":"Opening a chapter on non-action, the verse contrasts two daily courses; the Heshang Gong commentary, on chapter 48, reads learning as the study of government, instruction, rites and music, in which desire and adornment grow daily, and the Tao as their daily wearing away.","by":"the Heshang Gong commentary"},{"d":49,"at":[48],"ref":"Tao Te Ching 49","key":"The sage has in the world an appearance of indecision, and keeps his mind in a state of indifference to all. The people all keep their eyes and ears directed to him, and he deals with them all as his children.","s":"Closing a chapter on the sage and the people, the verse turns to his bearing; Wang Bi, on chapter 49, reads that indifference as examining nothing and seeking nothing, so the people, with nothing to evade or answer, all act from their true feelings.","by":"Wang Bi"},{"d":50,"at":[49],"ref":"Tao Te Ching 50","key":"The rhinoceros finds no place in him into which to thrust its horn, nor the tiger a place in which to fix its claws, nor the weapon a place to admit its point. And for what reason? Because there is in him no place of death.","s":"Closing a chapter on life and death, the verse ends with the one who guards life well; Wang Bi, on chapter 50, reads him as one who does not burden himself with desire or leave his root by seeking, and so cannot be harmed.","by":"Wang Bi"},{"d":51,"at":[50],"ref":"Tao Te Ching 51","key":"Thus it is that the Tao produces (all things), nourishes them, brings them to their full growth, nurses them, completes them, matures them, maintains them, and overspreads them.","s":"Restating the chapter’s opening, the verse lists eight works; the Heshang Gong commentary, on chapter 51, reads the Tao as not only giving life but rearing things and keeping their nature and life whole, as a ruler should in governing state and self.","by":"the Heshang Gong commentary"},{"d":52,"at":[51],"ref":"Tao Te Ching 52","key":"When the mother is found, we know what her children should be. When one knows that he is his mother’s child, and proceeds to guard (the qualities of) the mother that belong to him, to the end of his life he will be free from all peril.","s":"After naming the source of all under the sky their mother, the chapter turns to her children; Wang Bi, on chapter 52, reads the mother as root and the children as branches: the root grasped to know the branches, never forsaken to chase them.","by":"Wang Bi"},{"d":53,"at":[52],"ref":"Tao Te Ching 53","key":"If I were suddenly to become known, and (put into a position to) conduct (a government) according to the Great Tao, what I should be most afraid of would be a boastful display.","s":"Before decrying a court kept fine over empty granaries, the speaker imagines acting by the Great Tao; the Heshang Gong commentary, on chapter 53, takes the fear as dread of any deliberate measure, lest the Tao’s intent be lost: rewarding goodness may breed false goodness.","by":"the Heshang Gong commentary"},{"d":54,"at":[53],"ref":"Tao Te Ching 54","key":"In this way the effect will be seen in the person, by the observation of different cases; in the family; in the neighbourhood; in the state; and in the kingdom.","s":"Having traced the Tao from self to kingdom, the chapter turns to observation; the Heshang Gong commentary, on chapter 54, reads it as setting those who cultivate the Tao, from person to ruler, beside those who do not, to see which perish and which survive.","by":"the Heshang Gong commentary"},{"d":55,"at":[54],"ref":"Tao Te Ching 55","key":"He who has in himself abundantly the attributes (of the Tao) is like an infant. Poisonous insects will not sting him; fierce beasts will not seize him; birds of prey will not strike him.","s":"The chapter opens here, before the infant’s weak bones, firm grip and tireless crying; the Heshang Gong commentary, on chapter 55, reads it as the spirits guarding one who holds virtue as parents guard a newborn, which harms nothing and is harmed by nothing.","by":"the Heshang Gong commentary"},{"d":56,"at":[55],"ref":"Tao Te Ching 56","key":"He will blunt his sharp points and unravel the complications of things; he will attemper his brightness, and bring himself into agreement with the obscurity (of others).","s":"Describing one who knows yet keeps silent, the chapter lists his ways; Wang Bi, on chapter 56, reads the tempered brightness and shared obscurity as letting nothing stand out or be marked as base, so things have nothing to contend over or be ashamed of.","by":"Wang Bi"},{"d":57,"at":[56],"ref":"Tao Te Ching 57","key":"A state may be ruled by (measures of) correction; weapons of war may be used with crafty dexterity; (but) the kingdom is made one’s own (only) by freedom from action and purpose.","s":"Opening a chapter on prohibitions that breed poverty and theft, the text sets correction and war by craft beside inaction; Wang Bi, on chapter 57, reads rule by correction as laws aimed at the branches, leaving the root unestablished, which must end in crafty war.","by":"Wang Bi"},{"d":58,"at":[57],"ref":"Tao Te Ching 58","key":"Therefore the sage is (like) a square which cuts no one (with its angles); (like) a corner which injures no one (with its sharpness). He is straightforward, but allows himself no license; he is bright, but does not dazzle.","s":"Closing a chapter where correction turns to distortion, the text pictures the sage through paired contraries; Wang Bi, on chapter 58, reads it as guiding things away from crookedness without cutting them, and lighting what misleads them without hunting out their hidden faults.","by":"Wang Bi"},{"d":59,"at":[58],"ref":"Tao Te Ching 59","key":"For regulating the human (in our constitution) and rendering the (proper) service to the heavenly, there is nothing like moderation.","s":"The chapter opens with this counsel and ends on deep roots; the Heshang Gong commentary, on chapter 59, reads moderation as cherishing and sparing: a ruler sparing the people’s wealth, without extravagance, and one governing the self sparing his vital essence, without dissipation.","by":"the Heshang Gong commentary"},{"d":60,"at":[59],"ref":"Tao Te Ching 60","key":"Governing a great state is like cooking small fish.","s":"Opening a chapter on rule by the Tao, the likeness comes from the kitchen; the Heshang Gong commentary, on chapter 60, reads it as fish cooked ungutted, unscaled and unstirred lest they break, as fuss disorders a state and scatters a body’s vital essence.","by":"the Heshang Gong commentary"},{"d":61,"at":[60],"ref":"Tao Te Ching 61","key":"Each gets what it desires, but the great state must learn to abase itself.","s":"Closing a chapter on states that win one another by lowliness, the verse turns to the greater; Wang Bi, on chapter 61, reads it as a small state stooping only to keep itself whole, while all under heaven turns to a great state that stoops.","by":"Wang Bi"},{"d":62,"at":[61],"ref":"Tao Te Ching 62","key":"Even men who are not good are not abandoned by it.","s":"Calling the Tao a grace to good men and a guard to bad, the chapter goes further; the Heshang Gong commentary, on chapter 62, reads it as a charge to transform such men by the Tao, for before the Three Sovereigns none were cast off.","by":"the Heshang Gong commentary"},{"d":63,"at":[62],"ref":"Tao Te Ching 63","key":"Therefore the sage sees difficulty even in what seems easy, and so never has any difficulties.","s":"Ending a chapter on forestalling difficulty, the text returns to the sage; Wang Bi, on chapter 63, reads it as the sage, for all his gifts, still treating small, easy things as hard, which those without such gifts must heed all the more.","by":"Wang Bi"},{"d":64,"at":[63],"ref":"Tao Te Ching 64","key":"Therefore the sage desires what (other men) do not desire, and does not prize things difficult to get; he learns what (other men) do not learn, and turns back to what the multitude of men have passed by.","s":"The chapter ends by setting the sage apart from the many; the Heshang Gong commentary, on chapter 64, reads it as the sage learning what is so of itself and how to govern himself, where others learn cunning and how to govern the world.","by":"the Heshang Gong commentary"},{"d":65,"at":[64],"ref":"Tao Te Ching 65","key":"The ancients who showed their skill in practising the Tao did so, not to enlighten the people, but rather to make them simple and ignorant.","s":"Opening a chapter against governing by wisdom, the verse recalls the ancients; Wang Bi, on chapter 65, reads the enlightening as seeing much cunning, which clouds their simplicity, and the ignorance as having no knowledge, guarding the genuine and following what is so of itself.","by":"Wang Bi"},{"d":66,"at":[65],"ref":"Tao Te Ching 66","key":"In this way though he has his place above them, men do not feel his weight, nor though he has his place before them, do they feel it an injury to them.","s":"The chapter draws out what the sage’s lowliness brings; the Heshang Gong commentary, on chapter 66, reads it as the sage neither oppressing those below with rank nor overshadowing those behind with brightness, so the people love him as parents and wish him no harm.","by":"the Heshang Gong commentary"},{"d":67,"at":[66],"ref":"Tao Te Ching 67","key":"But I have three precious things which I prize and hold fast. The first is gentleness; the second is economy; and the third is shrinking from taking precedence of others.","s":"After answering what the world says of the speaker’s Tao, the chapter names his treasures; the Heshang Gong commentary, on chapter 67, reads the three as loving the people like infants, taxing as though taking from oneself, and keeping humble, never first to begin.","by":"the Heshang Gong commentary"},{"d":68,"at":[67],"ref":"Tao Te Ching 68","key":"He who vanquishes yet still Keeps from his foes apart; He whose hests men most fulfil Yet humbly plies his art.","s":"In a chapter on the skilled warrior, the verse turns to victory and command; the Heshang Gong commentary, on chapter 68, reads the victor as winning by benevolence and virtue, so foes yield without contest, and the good user of men as keeping below them.","by":"the Heshang Gong commentary"},{"d":69,"at":[68],"ref":"Tao Te Ching 69","key":"Thus it is that when opposing weapons are (actually) crossed, he who deplores (the situation) conquers.","s":"Closing a chapter on war that begins with a saying about yielding ground, the verse speaks of armies meeting; Wang Bi, on chapter 69, takes those who grieve as sure to cherish one another, neither chasing gain nor shunning harm, and so sure to win.","by":"Wang Bi"},{"d":70,"at":[69],"ref":"Tao Te Ching 70","key":"My words are very easy to know, and very easy to practise; but there is no one in the world who is able to know and able to practise them.","s":"In a chapter where few know the speaker, the verse opens on his words; Wang Bi, on chapter 70, takes them as known without leaving home and done without action, but restless desire keeps men from knowing them, and glory and profit from practising them.","by":"Wang Bi"},{"d":71,"at":[70],"ref":"Tao Te Ching 71","key":"To know and yet (think) we do not know is the highest (attainment); not to know (and yet think) we do know is a disease.","s":"Opening a short chapter on a sickness, the verse contrasts two ways of knowing; the Heshang Gong commentary, on chapter 71, reads it as knowing the Tao yet saying one does not, virtue at its height, and saying one knows it without knowing, virtue’s sickness.","by":"the Heshang Gong commentary"},{"d":72,"at":[71],"ref":"Tao Te Ching 72","key":"When the people do not fear what they ought to fear, that which is their great dread will come on them.","s":"Opening a chapter on the people and the sage, the verse gives a warning; Wang Bi, on chapter 72, takes it to mean that once stillness and humility give way to reliance on might, might no longer holds the people, and Heaven’s punishment comes.","by":"Wang Bi"},{"d":73,"at":[72],"ref":"Tao Te Ching 73","key":"It is the way of Heaven not to strive, and yet it skilfully overcomes; not to speak, and yet it is skilful in obtaining a reply; does not call, and yet men come to it of themselves.","s":"Weighing daring against not daring, the chapter turns to Heaven’s way; Wang Bi, on chapter 73, reads it as uncontested because it does not contend, answering with fortune for compliance and misfortune for defiance, and dwelling low, so things return of themselves.","by":"Wang Bi"},{"d":74,"at":[73],"ref":"Tao Te Ching 74","key":"There is always One who presides over the infliction of death. He who would inflict death in the room of him who so presides over it may be described as hewing wood instead of a great carpenter.","s":"Having questioned rule by fear of death, the chapter turns to who kills; the Heshang Gong commentary, on chapter 74, reads the One as Heaven, watching men’s faults, and a ruler killing in its stead as a clumsy workman, toiling in vain and wounding himself.","by":"the Heshang Gong commentary"},{"d":75,"at":[74],"ref":"Tao Te Ching 75","key":"The people are difficult to govern because of the (excessive) agency of their superiors (in governing them). It is through this that they are difficult to govern.","s":"Set between famine and making light of death, the verse names a second trouble; Wang Bi, on chapter 75, reads the chapter as tracing the people’s waywardness and the disorder of government to those above, not below, for the people follow those above.","by":"Wang Bi"},{"d":76,"at":[75],"ref":"Tao Te Ching 76","key":"Man at his birth is supple and weak; at his death, firm and strong.","s":"Opening a chapter that pairs softness with life, the verse begins with the body; the Heshang Gong commentary, on chapter 76, reads the living as supple while they hold harmonious breath, essence and spirit, and the dead as hard once these are gone.","by":"the Heshang Gong commentary"},{"d":77,"at":[76],"ref":"Tao Te Ching 77","key":"Who can take his own superabundance and therewith serve all under heaven? Only he who is in possession of the Tao!","s":"Having set Heaven’s way of evening out against man’s, the chapter asks and answers; Wang Bi, on chapter 77, reads the one who has the Tao as dwelling in fullness yet wholly empty, taking from what is to make up what is not.","by":"Wang Bi"},{"d":78,"at":[77],"ref":"Tao Te Ching 78","key":"Every one in the world knows that the soft overcomes the hard, and the weak the strong, but no one is able to carry it out in practice.","s":"From water, softest of things, the chapter turns to people; the Heshang Gong commentary, on chapter 78, reads it through water quenching fire and the tongue outlasting the teeth, and takes the failure to practise it as shame at humility and love of force.","by":"the Heshang Gong commentary"},{"d":79,"at":[78],"ref":"Tao Te Ching 79","key":"When a reconciliation is effected (between two parties) after a great animosity, there is sure to be a grudge remaining (in the mind of the one who was wrong).","s":"The chapter opens on this difficulty before turning to the sage’s half of the record; Wang Bi, on chapter 79, reads the animosity as born of an agreement not clearly settled, and the grudge as a wound that reconciling by virtue does not heal.","by":"Wang Bi"},{"d":80,"at":[79],"ref":"Tao Te Ching 80","key":"Though they had boats and carriages, they should have no occasion to ride in them; though they had buff coats and sharp weapons, they should have no occasion to don or use them.","s":"Picturing a little state of few people, the chapter turns to travel and arms; the Heshang Gong commentary, on chapter 80, reads the idle boats as stillness and non-action, fond of no pleasure trips, and the idle arms as no resentment anywhere under Heaven.","by":"the Heshang Gong commentary"},{"d":81,"at":[80],"ref":"Tao Te Ching 81","key":"The sage does not accumulate (for himself). The more that he expends for others, the more does he possess of his own; the more that he gives to others, the more does he have himself.","s":"The last chapter, after sayings on speech and knowing, turns here to the sage; the Heshang Gong commentary, on chapter 81, reads him as storing up virtue, not wealth: his virtue grows as he spreads it, his wealth as he gives it away.","by":"the Heshang Gong commentary"}]});
